The '''14th Street–Union Square station''' (also '''Union Square–14th Street''') is an express station on the [[IRT Lexington Avenue Line]]. The [[4 (New York City Subway service)|4]] and [[6 (New York City Subway service)|6]] trains stop here at all times;<ref name="tt4">{{NYCS const|timetable|4}}</ref><ref name="tt6">{{NYCS const|timetable|6}}</ref> the [[5 (New York City Subway service)|5]] train stops here at all times except late nights;<ref name="tt5">{{NYCS const|timetable|5}}</ref> and the [[6d (New York City Subway service)|&lt;6&gt;]] train stops here during weekdays in the peak direction.<ref name="tt6" /> The 5 train always makes express stops,<ref name="tt5" /> the 6 and &lt;6&gt; trains always make local stops;<ref name="tt6" /> and the 4 train makes express stops during the day and local stops at night.<ref name="tt4" /> The next station northbound is {{stl|NYCS|23rd Street–Baruch College|Lexington}} for local trains and {{stl|NYCS|Grand Central–42nd Street|Lexington}} for express trains, while the next station southbound is {{stl|NYCS|Astor Place}} for local trains and {{stl|NYCS|Brooklyn Bridge–City Hall}} for express trains.<ref name="submap">{{NYCS const|map}}</ref> When the subway opened, the next local stop to the north was {{stl|NYCS|18th Street|Lexington}};<ref name="145th" /> that station closed in 1948.<ref name="Closing">{{cite news |date=November 6, 1948 |title=IRT Station To Be Closed; East Side Subway Trains to End Stops at 18th Street |page=29 |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1948/11/06/archives/irt-station-to-be-closed-east-side-subway-trains-to-end-stops-at.html |url-status=live |access-date=April 17, 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180723065232/https://www.nytimes.com/1948/11/06/archives/irt-station-to-be-closed-east-side-subway-trains-to-end-stops-at.html |archive-date=July 23, 2018 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

The station has four tracks and two [[island platform]]s. The uptown and downtown platforms are offset from each other, having been extended at their rear ends, and are curved.<ref name="focus" />{{Rp|5}}<ref name="tracks" /> The island platforms allow for [[cross-platform interchange]]s between local and express trains heading in the same direction. Local trains use the outer tracks while express trains use the inner tracks.<ref name="tracks" /> The island platforms were originally {{convert|350|ft}} long, like the other express stations on the original IRT,<ref name="NYCL-1096" />{{Rp|4}}<ref name="HAER Designs" />{{rp|8}} but later were extended to {{convert|525|ft}}. The platforms are {{convert|30|ft|m}} wide at their widest point.<ref name="HAER Designs" />{{rp|8}} Departing passengers are asked to [[Mind the gap|watch the gap]] upon leaving the train at this station. [[Gap filler|Platform gap fillers]] on the downtown side use proximity sensors to detect when trains arrive, automatically extending when a train has stopped in the station.<ref name="focus" />{{Rp|5}}<ref name="Kolker 2016" /> Unlike the rest of the complex, no elevators lead down to the Lexington Avenue Line platforms.<ref name="Martinez 2021">{{cite web |last=Martinez |first=Jose |date=January 26, 2021 |title=Union Square Curves Add Accessibility Challenge to $100M Makeover |url=https://www.thecity.nyc/2021/1/25/22249712/union-square-curves-add-accessibility-challenge-to-100m-makeover |access-date=May 1, 2023 |website=The City |archive-date=December 6, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221206081057/https://www.thecity.nyc/2021/1/25/22249712/union-square-curves-add-accessibility-challenge-to-100m-makeover |url-status=live }}</ref> This is because the portions of the platforms under the mezzanine are too narrow for elevators.<ref name="nyt-2000-02-03" /> A 2020 study by [[Stantec]] found that, unless the downtown platform were rebuilt, it could never become fully accessible because of its "extreme curvature".<ref name="Martinez 2020">{{cite web |last=Martinez |first=Jose |date=February 20, 2020 |title=MTA Could Make Many More NYC Subway Stations Accessible, Study Finds |url=https://www.thecity.nyc/transportation/2020/2/20/21210518/mta-could-make-many-more-subway-stations-accessible-study-finds |access-date=May 1, 2023 |website=The City}}</ref> According to a separate report by engineering firm [[STV Inc.|STV]], it would be prohibitively expensive to relocate the southbound platform northward to eliminate the gap fillers.<ref name="Internet Archive 2023">{{cite web |date=March 25, 2023 |title=IRT Capacity Study Final Report |url=https://archive.org/details/irt-capacity-study-final-reportt-redacted |access-date=May 2, 2023 |website=Internet Archive |publisher=STV |page=A.16 }}</ref>

The station has two abandoned local [[side platform]]s; the northbound platform is visible through windows, bordered with wide, bright red frames.<ref name="focus" />{{Rp|5}}  A combination of island and side platforms was also used at [[Brooklyn Bridge–City Hall (IRT Lexington Avenue Line)|Brooklyn Bridge–City Hall]] on the IRT Lexington Avenue Line and [[96th Street (IRT Broadway–Seventh Avenue Line)|96th Street]] on the [[IRT Broadway–Seventh Avenue Line]].<ref name="HAER Designs">{{cite web|last=Framberger|first=David J.|date=1978|title=Architectural Designs for New York's First Subway|url=https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/ny/ny0300/ny0387/data/ny0387data.pdf|access-date=December 20, 2020|publisher=Historic American Engineering Record|pages=1–46 (PDF pp. 367–412) {{PD-notice}}|archive-date=January 17, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210117001227/https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/ny/ny0300/ny0387/data/ny0387data.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref>{{rp|8}}<!-- These side platforms were built to accommodate extra passenger volume and were built to the five-car length of the original IRT local trains. When trains were lengthened, the side platforms were deemed obsolete, and they were closed and walled off.-->

